            explanation: "Defining psychological disorders requires evaluating behaviour across four core dimensions — known universally in clinical psychology as the 'Four Ds': (1) Deviance: Behaviour, thoughts, and emotions that are extreme, unusual, bizarre, and depart significantly from societal norms and statistical averages; (2) Distress: Behaviour and feelings that are deeply unpleasant, agonizing, and upsetting to the individual and/or to others around them; (3) Dysfunction: Interference with the person's ability to carry out daily adaptive tasks, occupational duties, and interpersonal relationships constructively; and (4) Danger: Behaviour that poses a threat of self-harm, suicide, or violence toward others. Historically, abnormal behaviour was explained through three major lenses: (a) Supernatural & Magical Forces (evil spirits, demonic possession, exorcism, trephination/drilling skulls, witch hunts); (b) Biological/Organic Approach (belief that mental disorders arise from brain defects or humoral imbalances — Hippocrates proposed four humours: blood, black bile, yellow bile, phlegm; Galen); and (c) Psychological Approach (inadequate or maladaptive thinking, feeling, or perceiving). In the Middle Ages, St. Augustine wrote extensively on feelings and mental anguish. The Renaissance brought Johann Weyer, who emphasized psychological conflict and disturbed interpersonal relationships rather than witchcraft. The Age of Reason and Enlightenment (17th–18th centuries) fostered the Reform Movement led by Philippe Pinel in France and William Tuke in England (unchaining asylum inmates and introducing moral treatment) and Dorothea Dix in America, eventually culminating in the 20th-century deinstitutionalisation movement and the Biopsychosocial Model.",
