            id: 'psy-sec-7-4',
            title: 'Social Facilitation vs Social Loafing (Latane) & Group Polarisation Mechanisms',
            explanation: "Groups exert powerful effects on individual performance and decision-making: (1) Social Facilitation vs Social Loafing: While the mere presence of others can enhance performance on simple, well-practiced individual tasks through arousal (Social Facilitation), a markedly different phenomenon occurs in additive group tasks (such as a tug-of-war or collective clapping) where individual inputs are pooled into a single group product: Social Loafing. Social Loafing is a reduction in an individual's effort when working on a collective task compared to when working alone. Bibb Latane and his colleagues demonstrated this experimentally by blindfolding male students wearing headsets and asking them to clap or cheer as loudly as possible; when participants believed they were shouting in a group, the noise produced per person dropped drastically compared to when they shouted alone. Why does Social Loafing occur? (a) Group members feel less responsible for the overall task being performed and therefore exert less effort (diffusion of responsibility); (b) Motivation decreases because individuals realize their specific individual contributions will NOT be evaluated separately; (c) The performance of the group is not to be compared with other groups; (d) Improper coordination among members; and (e) Belonging to the group is not important to the members. How to Reduce Social Loafing: (a) Making the efforts of each individual identifiable; (b) Increasing the pressure to work hard (making group members committed to successful task performance); (c) Increasing the apparent importance or value of a task; (d) Making people feel that their individual contribution is unique and indispensable; and (e) Strengthening group cohesiveness which increases motivation for the group to succeed. (2) Group Polarisation: Groups are far more likely to take extreme decisions (either toward extreme risk — the 'Risky Shift' — or toward extreme caution) than individuals deciding alone. Group Polarisation refers to the strengthening of a group's initial position as a consequence of group interaction and discussion (e.g., if a group initially favours capital punishment mildly, post-discussion they will advocate for it vehemently). Why does Group Polarisation occur? Three mechanisms operate: (a) Informational Influence (Persuasive Arguments): In the company of like-minded people, you hear newer, compelling arguments favouring your initial viewpoint; (b) Normative Influence (Bandwagon Effect): When you find others also favouring your view, you feel your view is validated by the public, creating a bandwagon effect; and (c) Ingroup Identification: When you perceive others as having similar views, you categorize them as ingroup and start conforming even more strongly to their extreme position to prove your loyalty.",
